{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-126521" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the arc of the curve $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ that lies between the lines $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-126521" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\sqrt{1+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-126521" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f3f4505ff8d1d6bf54bfc5a20db0c3ac75b5341d79e696d1870a88e4c887b78a"} {"question" "q-126521" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-126521" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡x\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡x\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-126521" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-109126" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/assets/0edb5218c2d1527957053a35e992c7538a8857132a6d48114e16e5f71d3848d6.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $y=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}$ that lies in the first quadrant between the line $x=0$ and its $x$-intercept. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-109126" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $x$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 4-{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}})\\sqrt{1+{(-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}\\,\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-109126" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f3f4505ff8d1d6bf54bfc5a20db0c3ac75b5341d79e696d1870a88e4c887b78a"} {"question" "q-109126" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-109126" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(4-{x}^{2})\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-109126" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}x"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-109143" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the arc of the curve $y=2-\\sqrt{x}$ that lies between the lines $x=0$ and $x=4.$ What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-109143" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${x}_{1}=0$ to ${x}_{2}=4.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2-\\sqrt{x})=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}(2-\\sqrt{x})\\sqrt{1+{(-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}(2-\\sqrt{x})\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{4x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}(2-\\sqrt{x})\\sqrt{\\frac{4x+1}{x}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-109143" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f3f4505ff8d1d6bf54bfc5a20db0c3ac75b5341d79e696d1870a88e4c887b78a"} {"question" "q-109143" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-109143" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}(2-\\sqrt{x})\\sqrt{\\frac{4x+1}{x}}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}(2-\\sqrt{x})\\sqrt{4x+1}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}(2-\\sqrt{x})\\sqrt{\\frac{4x+1}{x}}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{(2-x)\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+1}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-109143" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}(2-\\sqrt{x})\\sqrt{\\frac{4x+1}{x}}\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-109123" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/assets/924444fa2da1709d788d3b3522e86743b46ee1c5a25999bb77731449e621a07d.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $y=1-(x+1)^{2}$ that lies in the second quadrant between its $x$-intercepts. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-109123" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $x$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-(x+1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ x+1 & =\\pm 1 \\\\ x & =-1\\pm 1 \\\\ x & =-2,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-2$ to $x=0.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1-(x+1)^{2})=-2x-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}(1-(x+1)^{2})\\sqrt{1+(-2x-2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}(-{x}^{2}-2x)\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+8x+5}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-109123" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f3f4505ff8d1d6bf54bfc5a20db0c3ac75b5341d79e696d1870a88e4c887b78a"} {"question" "q-109123" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-109123" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+8x+5}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "-2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}(x+1)^{2}\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+8x+5}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}(-{x}^{2}-2x)\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+8x+5}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "4\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}(-x-1)\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+5x}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "4\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}(-{x}^{2}-2x)\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+5x}\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-109123" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}(-{x}^{2}-2x)\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+8x+5}\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-109124" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the arc of the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ that lies between the lines $x=1$ and $x=e.$ What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-109124" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${x}_{1}=1$ to ${x}_{2}=e.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{1}{x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-109124" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f3f4505ff8d1d6bf54bfc5a20db0c3ac75b5341d79e696d1870a88e4c887b78a"} {"question" "q-109124" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-109124" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\,\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\sqrt{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-109124" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-109155" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/assets/22bb16a874005f09d5cbad055f149f07e2139f559a2a04368c32696567714b26.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $x=\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}}$ that lies in the first quadrant between the line $y=0$ and its $y$-intercept. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-109155" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 2-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =2 \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=\\sqrt{2}.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}y\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}y\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{2-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi \\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-109155" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f3f4505ff8d1d6bf54bfc5a20db0c3ac75b5341d79e696d1870a88e4c887b78a"} {"question" "q-109155" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-109155" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\pi \\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "2\\pi \\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "\\pi \\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}}}{y}\\,\\text{d}y"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-109155" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\pi \\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-104533" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the arc of the curve $x=\\operatorname{tan}⁡y$ that lies between $y=0$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-104533" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${y}_{1}=0$ to ${y}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡y)={sec}^{2}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}y\\sqrt{1+{({sec}^{2}⁡y)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}y\\sqrt{1+{sec}^{4}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-104533" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f3f4505ff8d1d6bf54bfc5a20db0c3ac75b5341d79e696d1870a88e4c887b78a"} {"question" "q-104533" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-104533" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡y\\sqrt{1+{sec}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{1+{sec}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}y\\sqrt{1+{csc}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}y\\sqrt{1+{sec}^{4}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}y\\sqrt{1+{tan}^{4}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-104533" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}y\\sqrt{1+{sec}^{4}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-109188" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/assets/9b27f44aa7ef6bef95a88ccfcb6495d17e9457a7452bf602f87dcd5b95ec71dc.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ that lies in the first quadrant between its $y$-intercept and the line $y=e.$ What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-109188" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y} & ={e}^{0} \\\\ y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $y=1$ to $y=e.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=\\frac{1}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{1}{y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}y\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-109188" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f3f4505ff8d1d6bf54bfc5a20db0c3ac75b5341d79e696d1870a88e4c887b78a"} {"question" "q-109188" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-109188" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\sqrt{y({y}^{2}+1)}\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}y\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{e}\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{e}y\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}y"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-109188" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}y"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-109179" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/assets/dc94ee46e2b9381afb6f62b74c67d76d137f857c3f78f36df2fdad5661b114b3.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $x=2y-{y}^{3}$ that lies in the first quadrant between its $y$-intercepts. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-109179" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y-{y}^{3} & =0 \\\\ y(2-{y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=\\sqrt{2}.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(2y-{y}^{3})=2-3{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(2-3{y}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}y\\sqrt{9{y}^{4}-12{y}^{2}+5}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-109179" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f3f4505ff8d1d6bf54bfc5a20db0c3ac75b5341d79e696d1870a88e4c887b78a"} {"question" "q-109179" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-109179" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{1+2y-{y}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\sqrt{9{y}^{4}-12{y}^{2}+5}\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}(2y-{y}^{3})\\sqrt{9{y}^{4}-12{y}^{2}+5}\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{2y-{y}^{3}}{\\sqrt{1+2y-{y}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}y\\sqrt{9{y}^{4}-12{y}^{2}+5}\\,\\text{d}y"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-109179" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}y\\sqrt{9{y}^{4}-12{y}^{2}+5}\\,\\text{d}y"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-126551" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/assets/1204968a7d4eee9f299b432c21441f670c7ebfab0629da6ef66a7960033a9c1b.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $x={y}^{2}-2y$ that lies in the second quadrant between its $y$-intercepts. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-126551" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-2y & =0 \\\\ y(y-2) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2}-2y)=2y-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\sqrt{1+{(2y-2)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\sqrt{4{y}^{2}-8y+5}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-126551" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f3f4505ff8d1d6bf54bfc5a20db0c3ac75b5341d79e696d1870a88e4c887b78a"} {"question" "q-126551" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-126551" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{2}-2y)\\sqrt{4{y}^{2}-8y+5}\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\sqrt{4{y}^{2}-8y+1}\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}({y}^{2}-2y)\\sqrt{4{y}^{2}+5}\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\sqrt{4{y}^{2}+5}\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\sqrt{4{y}^{2}-8y+5}\\,\\text{d}y"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-126551" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\sqrt{4{y}^{2}-8y+5}\\,\\text{d}y"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-353403" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the arc of the curve $y=\\sqrt{3x+1}$ that lies between the lines $x=-\\frac{1}{3}$ and $x=1.$ Find the surface area $S$ generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$S=$ \n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-353403" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3}$ to ${x}_{2}=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{3x+1})=\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3x+1}}\\,⇒\\,{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}=\\frac{9}{4(3x+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-1/3}^{1}\\sqrt{3x+1}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{9}{4(3x+1)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-1/3}^{1}\\sqrt{3x+1}\\sqrt{\\frac{12x+13}{4(3x+1)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-1/3}^{1}\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{12x+13}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/3}^{1}\\sqrt{12x+13}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot \\frac{(12x+13)^{3/2}}{18}\\,{|}_{-1/3}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{18}({25}^{3/2}-{9}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{49\\pi }{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-353403" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f3f4505ff8d1d6bf54bfc5a20db0c3ac75b5341d79e696d1870a88e4c887b78a"} {"question" "q-353403" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-353403" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{49\\pi}{9}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-126589" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the arc of the curve $x=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-1$ that lies between the lines $y=0$ and $y=1.$ Calculate the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-126589" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${y}_{1}=0$ to ${y}_{2}=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-1)=y\\,⇒\\,{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\sqrt{1+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the substitution \n\n$$\nu={y}^{2}+1\\,⇒\\,(\\frac{1}{2})\\text{d}u=y\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\sqrt{1+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{u}\\,(\\frac{1}{2})\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\pi {\\frac{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}{3}\\,|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}(\\sqrt{{2}^{3}}-\\sqrt{{1}^{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}(2\\sqrt{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-126589" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f3f4505ff8d1d6bf54bfc5a20db0c3ac75b5341d79e696d1870a88e4c887b78a"} {"question" "q-126589" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-126589" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\pi (\\sqrt{2}-1)"] ["math" "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}(2\\sqrt{2}-1)"] ["math" "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}\\,\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\,\\pi }{3}"] ["math" "\\pi (3\\sqrt{2}-1)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-126589" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}(2\\sqrt{2}-1)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-353405" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the arc of the curve $y=2x+1$ that lies between the lines $x=0$ and $x=1.$ Find the surface area $S$ generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$S=$ \n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-353405" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${x}_{1}=0$ to ${x}_{2}=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x+1)=2\\,⇒\\,{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+1)\\sqrt{1+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+1)\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi \\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi \\sqrt{5}({x}^{2}+x){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\pi \\sqrt{5}((1+1)-0) \\\\  & =4\\pi \\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-353405" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f3f4505ff8d1d6bf54bfc5a20db0c3ac75b5341d79e696d1870a88e4c887b78a"} {"question" "q-353405" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-353405" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4\\pi\\sqrt{5}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-109319" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the arc of the curve $x=\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}}$ that lies between the lines $y=0$ and $y=2.$ Calculate the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-109319" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${y}_{1}=0$ to ${y}_{2}=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}}}\\,⇒\\,{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9-{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{9-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\frac{3y}{\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =3\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{2y}{\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the substitution \n\n$$\nu=9-{y}^{2}\\,⇒\\,\\text{d}u=-2y\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =3\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{2y}{\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-3\\pi {\\int }_{9}^{5}{u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\pi {\\int }_{5}^{9}{u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\pi {[2\\sqrt{u}\\,]}_{5}^{9} \\\\  & =6\\pi (3-\\sqrt{5}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-109319" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f3f4505ff8d1d6bf54bfc5a20db0c3ac75b5341d79e696d1870a88e4c887b78a"} {"question" "q-109319" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-109319" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\pi (6-\\sqrt{5})"] ["math" "4\\pi (2-\\sqrt{3})"] ["math" "6\\pi (3-\\sqrt{5})"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{2}(5-\\sqrt{3})"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{4}(2-\\sqrt{5})"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-109319" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6\\pi (3-\\sqrt{5})"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-353411" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the arc of the curve $x=\\frac{4}{3}{y}^{3/2}$ that lies between the lines $y=0$ and $y=2.$ Calculate the surface area $S$ generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$S=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-353411" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${y}_{1}=0$ to ${y}_{2}=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\frac{4}{3}{y}^{3/2})=2\\sqrt{y}\\,⇒\\,{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}=4y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\sqrt{1+4y}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the substitution \n\n$$\nu=1+4y\\,⇒\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}y,\\,y=\\frac{u-1}{4},\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\sqrt{1+4y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{9}\\frac{u-1}{4}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}{\\int }_{1}^{9}(u-1)\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}{\\int }_{1}^{9}({u}^{3/2}-{u}^{1/2})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}(\\frac{2}{5}{u}^{5/2}-\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2})\\,{|}_{1}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}((\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {9}^{5/2}-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {9}^{3/2})-(\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {1}^{5/2}-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {1}^{3/2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}((\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot 243-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 27)-(\\frac{2}{5}-\\frac{2}{3})) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}(\\frac{396}{5}+\\frac{4}{15}) \\\\  & =\\frac{149\\pi }{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-353411" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f3f4505ff8d1d6bf54bfc5a20db0c3ac75b5341d79e696d1870a88e4c887b78a"} {"question" "q-353411" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "q-353411" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{149\\pi}{15}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/state.json" "file_sha256" "169162add2d7c7231c9dd4a7424aa3a14e817d1cba38f9cf6107b3b405c1862b"} {"question" "e-8905" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the arc of the curve $y=e^x$ that lies between the lines $x=0$ and $x=2.$ What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/example-8905.json" "file_sha256" "2f4aedb509775844e06e77f7c5db63a6e8e931b5e034157db1338cbf451c21cf"} {"question" "e-8905" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi y\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y = f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{x_1}^{x_2} y \\sqrt{1+ \\left( \\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\right)^2} \\: \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $x_1=0$ to $x_2={2}.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})={e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{x}\\sqrt{1+({e}^{x})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{x}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/example-8905.json" "file_sha256" "2f4aedb509775844e06e77f7c5db63a6e8e931b5e034157db1338cbf451c21cf"} {"question" "e-9120" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the arc of the curve $x=y^3-y$ that lies in the second quadrant between its $y$-intercepts. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/assets/1bdbe21cbb517645dfe9362f3313c2d85118acfea1d839fe33ae9b9ac6c3ed50.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/example-9120.json" "file_sha256" "996c7c00c90f5f3ab1a6d4a93cf37370a5cc8fa38c4269833d0b6ef6bfae46a2"} {"question" "e-9120" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi y\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x = f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d y}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{y_1}^{y_2} y \\sqrt{1+ \\left( \\dfrac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} \\right)^2} \\: \\textrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{3}-y & =0 \\\\ y({y}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{3}-y)=3{y}^{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\sqrt{1+{(3{y}^{2}-1)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\sqrt{2-6{y}^{2}+9{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/example-9120.json" "file_sha256" "996c7c00c90f5f3ab1a6d4a93cf37370a5cc8fa38c4269833d0b6ef6bfae46a2"} {"question" "e-8909" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the arc of the curve $y=\\sqrt{x-2}$ that lies between the lines $x=2$ and $x=4.$ Calculate the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/example-8909.json" "file_sha256" "0a6e9f17439b7d3c6ddd341b2d9979e6682ebde4eb0721e395bcafa7ebb22a8f"} {"question" "e-8909" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi y\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y = f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{x_1}^{x_2} y \\sqrt{1+ \\left( \\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\right)^2} \\: \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $x_1=2$ to $x_2=4.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x-2})=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x-2}}\\,⇒\\,{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4(x-2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{x-2}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{4(x-2)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{x-2}\\sqrt{\\frac{4x-7}{4(x-2)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{x-2}(\\frac{\\sqrt{4x-7}}{2\\sqrt{x-2}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{4x-7}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the substitution \n\n$$\nu=4x-7 \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad \\textrm du = 4\\,\\textrm dx,\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{4x-7}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}{\\int }_{1}^{9}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}\\,|}_{1}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}(27-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{13\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/example-8909.json" "file_sha256" "0a6e9f17439b7d3c6ddd341b2d9979e6682ebde4eb0721e395bcafa7ebb22a8f"}] "usage" {"e-8909" [] "q-104533" [] "e-9120" [] "e-8905" [] "q-109179" []} "basis" 2857 "retractions" [[17592186129347 :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $x = \\tan y$ that lies between $y = 0$ and $y = \\frac{π}{4}$. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2π$ radians about the $x$-axis?"] [17592186129349 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1994/Source/Images/q-109179.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $x = 2y - y^{3}$ that lies in the first quadrant between its $y$-intercepts. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2π$ radians about the $x$-axis?"] [17592186128969 :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $y=e^x$ that lies between the lines $x=0$ and $x=2$. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis?"] [17592186128969 :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi y\\,\\textrm d s\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y = f(x)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{x_1}^{x_2} y \\sqrt{1+ \\left(\\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\right)^2} \\: \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $x_1=0$ to $x_2={2}$.\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(e^{x}) = e^{x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= 2π∫_{x_{1}}^{x_{2}}y\\sqrt{1 + (\\frac{dy}{dx})^{2}}dx \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{0}^{2}e^{x}\\sqrt{1 + (e^{x})^{2}}dx \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{0}^{2}e^{x}\\sqrt{1 + e^{2x}}dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186128971 :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $x=y^3-y$ that lies in the second quadrant between its $y$-intercepts. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1994/Source/Images/2e7b78757d93bacc9371bde4d7088279.png)"] [17592186128971 :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi y\\,\\textrm d s\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x = f(y)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d y}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d y\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{y_1}^{y_2} y \\sqrt{1+ \\left(\\dfrac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} \\right)^2} \\: \\textrm{d}y\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{3} - y &= 0 \\\\\ny(y^{2} - 1) &= 0 \\\\\ny &= 0, ± 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=1$.\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dx}{dy} &= \\frac{d}{dy}(y^{3} - y) = 3y^{2} - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= 2π∫_{y_{1}}^{y_{2}}y\\sqrt{1 + (\\frac{dx}{dy})^{2}}dy \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{0}^{1}y\\sqrt{1 + (3y^{2} - 1)^{2}}dy \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{0}^{1}y\\sqrt{2 - 6y^{2} + 9y^{4}}dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186128970 :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $y=\\sqrt{x-2}$ that lies between the lines $x=2$ and $x=4$. Calculate the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis."] [17592186128970 :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi y\\,\\textrm d s\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y = f(x)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{x_1}^{x_2} y \\sqrt{1+ \\left(\\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\right)^2} \\: \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $x_1=2$ to $x_2=4$.\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\sqrt{x - 2}) = \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x - 2}}⇒(\\frac{dy}{dx})^{2} = (1)/(4(x - 2))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= 2π∫_{x_{1}}^{x_{2}}y\\sqrt{1 + (\\frac{dy}{dx})^{2}}dx \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{x - 2}\\sqrt{1 + (1)/(4(x - 2))}dx \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{x - 2}\\sqrt{(4x - 7)/(4(x - 2))}dx \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{x - 2}(\\frac{\\sqrt{4x - 7}}{2\\sqrt{x - 2}})dx \\\\\n&= π∫_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{4x - 7}dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the substitution\n\n$$\nu=4x-7 \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad \\textrm du = 4\\,\\textrm dx\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= π∫_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{4x - 7}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{4}∫_{1}^{9}\\sqrt{u}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{4} \\cdot \\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{u^{3}} \\mid _{1}^{9} \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{6}(27 - 1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{13π}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186125889 17592186125890 17592186125891 17592186125892 17592186125893 17592186125901 17592186125902 17592186125903 17592186125904 17592186125905] "immutable_fields" [[17592186125888 :answer-field/correct] [17592186125888 :answer-field/id] [17592186125888 :answer-field/key] [17592186125888 :answer-field/type] [17592186125900 :answer-field/correct] [17592186125900 :answer-field/id] [17592186125900 :answer-field/key] [17592186125900 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
